                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "evolution":</h3><p><b>Evolution</b> is a line of microphones manufactured by Sennheiser, essentially to compete with Shure for the stage-microphone market. The Evolution mics are manufactured with a high degree of automation. Many, if not all are built in Sennheiser&#8217;s plant in Wennebostel, Germany.</p><p>The first-generation <b>evolution</b> series, e600 and e800, were introduced in 1998, and many of the current mics in this series all date to that initial release. Some have been redesigned; these are indicated by a &#8220;II&#8221; in their model names, like the e 602-II. </p><p>The e600 series are primarily instrument microphones. The e800 series are primarily vocal microphones. </p><p>The e900 series is newer, and contains a mix of vocal and instrument microphones. These are the premium microphones in the <b>evolution</b> series, and can be assumed to have been designed for superior performance in live sound applications.</p>
